# Why Your Website Isn't Showing on Google

You've built your website, published your pages, connected your domain, and shared your link with a few people.

Everything seems to be working.

But when you open Google and search for your business name, website, or services, nothing appears.

You try different search terms, scroll through the results, and still can't find your website.

So, what's happening?

If your website isn't showing on Google, there could be several reasons. Google may not have discovered your website yet, your pages may not have been indexed, or your website may simply not rank high enough for the searches you're performing.

These are different problems, and each requires a different solution.

In this guide, we'll explain why your website may not be appearing on Google, how to identify the problem, and what you can do to improve its visibility.

## First, Is Your Website Missing From Google or Just Not Ranking?

Before trying to fix anything, it's important to understand two terms: indexing and ranking.

They are related, but they don't mean the same thing.

### What Does It Mean for a Website to Be Indexed?

Indexing happens when Google processes a page and stores information about it in its search index.

Think of Google's index as a huge collection of information about webpages that Google may show in search results.

If your page hasn't been indexed, it generally cannot appear in Google's normal search results.

### What Does It Mean for a Website to Rank?

Ranking refers to where your website appears for a particular search query.

For example, if someone searches for "website designer in Lagos" and your website appears on the fifth page, it may be indexed but have limited visibility for that search.

This distinction matters.

**If your website isn't indexed, you need to investigate why Google hasn't included it in its index. If it's indexed but not ranking well, you need to work on its relevance, content, authority, and overall search performance.**

Don't assume every visibility problem is an indexing problem.

## How to Check If Your Website Is on Google

There are two simple ways to start checking.

### Method 1: Search for Your Website on Google

Open Google and search:

`site:yourdomain.com`

Replace `yourdomain.com` with your actual domain.

For example:

`site:emmystack01.com`

Google may show pages from your website that it has indexed.

You can also search for a specific page:

`site:yourdomain.com/your-page`

This is a quick way to investigate whether Google has indexed some of your pages.

However, search operators are not a complete or definitive indexing report. Google recommends using Search Console for a more reliable diagnosis.

### Method 2: Use Google Search Console

Google Search Console is a free tool that helps website owners monitor their website's presence in Google Search.

It provides information about:

- Indexed and non-indexed pages
- Search queries that bring visitors to your website
- Impressions and clicks
- Technical indexing issues
- Sitemap submissions
- Mobile and other search-related reports

Once your website is connected and verified, you can use the URL Inspection tool to check individual pages.

Enter the URL of the page you want to investigate.

Search Console will help you understand whether Google knows about the URL, whether it has been crawled, and whether it is indexed.

You can also run a live URL test to check how Google can access the current version of the page.

Visit the official [Google Search Console](https://search.google.com/search-console/about) to get started.

## 10 Reasons Your Website Isn't Showing on Google

Now that you understand indexing and ranking, let's look at some common reasons your website may be missing from search results.

### 1. Your Website Is Too New

One of the simplest explanations is that your website was recently launched.

Google does not necessarily discover and index a new website immediately after publication.

It needs to discover the website, crawl its pages, process their content, and decide whether to include them in its index.

This process can take days or longer, depending on the website and other circumstances.

If your website was launched yesterday and you cannot find it on Google today, that doesn't automatically mean something is broken.

**What you can do:**

- Verify your website in Google Search Console.
- Submit your XML sitemap.
- Use URL Inspection to check your homepage.
- Request indexing for important published pages.
- Make sure your website is accessible to search engines.

Then monitor the results rather than repeatedly submitting the same URLs.

Requesting indexing is not a guarantee that Google will index your website immediately or at all.

### 2. Google Hasn't Discovered Your Website

Your website may be publicly accessible, but Google might not know that it exists.

Search engines discover pages through links, sitemaps, and other discovery methods.

If your website is new and has very few links pointing to it, discovery may take longer.

**What you can do:**

- Submit your sitemap through Google Search Console.
- Link important pages from your homepage.
- Make sure your website has clear navigation.
- Link to your website from relevant public profiles and business listings.
- Create useful content that people may naturally reference and share.

For example, if your homepage links to your Services, About, and Contact pages, it gives visitors and search engines a clearer path to discover those sections.

Don't rely on a sitemap alone. Your website should also have a logical internal linking structure.

### 3. Your Website Is Blocking Google From Indexing It

This is one of the technical problems worth checking carefully.

Websites can contain instructions that tell search engines not to index certain pages.

One example is a `noindex` directive.

A page with a `noindex` instruction tells Google not to include that page in its search index.

This can happen unintentionally during development.

For example, a developer may use a setting that prevents search engines from indexing a website while it is being built. If that setting remains active after launch, the website may continue blocking indexing.

**What you can do:**

- Check whether your important pages contain a `noindex` directive.
- Review your website's SEO or search visibility settings.
- Check the Indexing section of URL Inspection in Search Console.
- Ask your developer to investigate if you are unsure how to fix it.

One important distinction: `robots.txt` and `noindex` do different jobs.

A `robots.txt` file controls crawling, while `noindex` tells search engines not to index a page.

Blocking a page in `robots.txt` does not reliably remove it from Google's index. In fact, it may prevent Google from seeing a `noindex` instruction on that page.

If you want a page indexed, make sure Google can crawl it and that it does not contain an unintended indexing restriction.

### 4. Your Website Has Technical Errors

Sometimes Google cannot access your website or certain pages properly because of technical problems.

Examples include:

- Server errors
- Broken URLs
- Incorrect redirects
- Pages returning 404 errors
- Hosting problems
- Pages requiring a login
- Incorrect server responses

For Google to index a page, it generally needs to be publicly accessible, return a successful HTTP response, and contain indexable content.

If Google encounters an error when trying to access an important page, that page may not be indexed.

**What you can do:**

- Check that your website loads without errors.
- Test important pages individually.
- Make sure your domain points to the correct hosting environment.
- Check redirects and broken links.
- Review indexing reports in Google Search Console.
- Ask your developer to investigate server-side problems.

A website that works on your own device may still have accessibility problems that need technical investigation.

### 5. Your Website Has Poor or Unhelpful Content

Being technically accessible does not automatically make a page worthy of appearing in search results.

Google needs to understand what your page is about and whether it provides useful information.

Imagine you run a digital marketing business, but your homepage only says:

"Welcome to our website. We provide quality services. Contact us today."

A visitor may struggle to understand exactly what you offer, who you serve, or why they should choose you.

Search engines may also have difficulty understanding the page's purpose.

**What you can do:**

- Clearly explain your products and services.
- Create dedicated pages for important services.
- Answer questions your potential customers actually ask.
- Provide useful details instead of generic statements.
- Keep your information accurate and relevant.
- Avoid copying content from other websites.

Your website should help visitors understand your business, not simply contain words added to fill space.

### 6. Your Website Is Targeting the Wrong Keywords

You may have a website that is indexed but doesn't appear when you search for the terms you expect.

One possible reason is that your pages are targeting search terms that don't match how your potential customers search.

For example, you might describe your service using an internal business term that customers rarely use.

Someone looking for your service may search for a completely different phrase.

**What you can do:**

- Research the words your potential customers use.
- Include relevant terms naturally in your page titles and headings.
- Create content that matches the purpose behind a search.
- Use specific phrases where they accurately describe your offer.
- Review the queries shown in Google Search Console.

Don't force keywords into every paragraph. Your content should sound natural and provide useful information.

### 7. Your Website Has Very Little Internal Linking

Internal links connect one page on your website to another.

They help visitors navigate your content and give search engines additional paths to discover your pages.

Imagine you publish ten useful articles, but none of them link to each other or to your important service pages.

Visitors may struggle to discover related content, and search engines may have fewer clear paths to follow.

**What you can do:**

- Link related articles to one another.
- Link relevant articles to your service pages.
- Make sure important pages are accessible from your navigation.
- Use descriptive link text.
- Avoid creating unnecessary links just to increase their number.

For example, an article about website costs can naturally link to your website development services page.

The link should help readers take the next step, not simply exist for SEO.

### 8. Your Website Has Duplicate or Confusing URLs

Sometimes the same or very similar content can be accessible through multiple URLs.

For example, a page might be available through different URL variations.

If Google discovers several versions of similar content, it may choose one URL as the canonical version and treat the others as duplicates or alternatives.

This is not always a problem. Many websites naturally have duplicate URL variations that should not all appear in search results.

However, incorrect canonical settings can cause Google to select a different URL from the one you intended.

**What you can do:**

- Use consistent URL formats.
- Set appropriate canonical URLs.
- Avoid publishing unnecessary duplicate pages.
- Check the Google-selected canonical in URL Inspection.
- Use redirects when permanently moving pages.

If your website has several URL variations, ask your developer to review the canonical and redirect configuration.

### 9. Your Website Is Competing in a Very Crowded Search Market

Your website may be indexed and technically healthy but still struggle to appear prominently for competitive searches.

Imagine you run a business that offers website design services in Nigeria.

You are competing with established agencies, freelancers, directories, and other websites that have been publishing relevant content for years.

Simply creating a page titled "Website Design in Nigeria" does not guarantee that your website will outrank them.

**What you can do:**

- Create genuinely useful service pages.
- Publish content that answers customer questions.
- Explain your experience and approach.
- Build relevant relationships and earn genuine mentions.
- Improve your website's overall quality.
- Target specific, relevant searches where appropriate.

You don't have to compete for every broad keyword immediately.

A focused page that answers a specific customer need may be a more practical starting point than trying to rank for an extremely competitive search.

### 10. Your Website Has Recently Changed

Have you recently redesigned your website, changed your domain, renamed pages, or moved content?

These changes can affect how Google discovers and processes your URLs.

For example, if you change a page URL without redirecting the old address, visitors and search engines may encounter a broken page.

Similarly, changing your website's domain without proper migration planning can create indexing and visibility problems.

**What you can do:**

- Set up appropriate redirects for permanently moved pages.
- Update internal links.
- Submit the new sitemap.
- Verify the correct domain property in Search Console.
- Check important URLs using URL Inspection.
- Monitor indexing reports after the changes.

Website migrations should be planned carefully, especially when the existing website already receives search traffic.

## How to Get Your Website Showing on Google

If you've identified an issue, here's a practical checklist to help you move forward.

### Step 1: Make Sure Your Website Is Publicly Accessible

Open your website in a private browser window or on another device.

Check that visitors can access it without logging in.

Also check that important pages load properly.

### Step 2: Set Up Google Search Console

Verify ownership of your website in Google Search Console.

This gives you access to indexing reports, URL Inspection, sitemap submissions, and search performance information.

Make sure you select and verify the appropriate version of your website, including the relevant domain and URL variations.

### Step 3: Submit Your XML Sitemap

A sitemap helps Google discover important URLs on your website.

Many website platforms and content management systems generate sitemaps automatically.

Find your sitemap, check that it is accessible, and submit it through the Sitemaps section of Search Console.

A sitemap helps with discovery, but it does not guarantee that every listed page will be crawled or indexed.

### Step 4: Inspect Your Important Pages

Use the URL Inspection tool to check your homepage and important service pages.

Look for information such as:

- Whether Google knows about the URL
- Whether crawling is allowed
- Whether indexing is allowed
- The last crawl date
- The canonical URL selected by Google

If you find an issue, investigate the specific reason rather than submitting the page repeatedly.

### Step 5: Fix Technical Problems

Check for unintended `noindex` directives, blocked resources, server errors, broken links, incorrect redirects, and other technical issues.

If you are not comfortable working with website settings or code, ask your developer to help.

### Step 6: Improve Your Website Content

Review your homepage, service pages, product pages, and articles.

Ask yourself:

- Is it clear what my business does?
- Does each important page have a clear purpose?
- Does the content answer real questions?
- Are the page titles descriptive?
- Can visitors find related information easily?
- Is the information accurate and up to date?

### Step 7: Request Indexing Where Appropriate

After fixing an issue or publishing an important page, you can use URL Inspection to request indexing.

Google will decide when to crawl the page and whether to index it.

You don't need to request indexing for every small change, and repeatedly submitting an unchanged URL will not make it rank faster.

### Step 8: Monitor Your Progress

Continue checking Google Search Console.

Look at:

- Pages indexed
- Pages not indexed and their reported reasons
- Search impressions
- Clicks
- Search queries
- Changes in search performance

This helps you understand whether your website is becoming more visible and where further improvements may be needed.

## How Long Does It Take for Google to Show Your Website?

There is no guaranteed timeline.

Some websites and pages may be discovered and indexed within days, while others may take weeks or longer.

Requesting indexing can help Google become aware of an important page, but it does not guarantee immediate inclusion.

It is also important to remember that indexing and ranking are different.

Your website might be indexed but still not appear prominently for the keywords you want.

Improving search visibility requires more than getting your pages into Google's index. You also need relevant content, a technically sound website, and a good experience for visitors.

If you're still planning your website, our guide on [how long it takes to build a website](/articles/how-long-does-it-take-to-build-a-website) explains the development timeline. Getting your website launched is one milestone; building its search visibility is another.

## Does Paying Someone Guarantee Your Website Will Appear on Google?

No.

Hiring an SEO professional or web developer can help you identify technical issues, improve your content, and implement an SEO strategy.

However, nobody can guarantee that Google will index every page or rank your website in a specific position.

Be cautious of providers who promise:

- Guaranteed first-page rankings
- Instant indexing for every page
- Thousands of backlinks in a few days
- Permanent top positions on Google
- Guaranteed traffic within a fixed period

A responsible provider should explain the work being done, the expected outcomes, and the limitations involved.

You should also understand what you are paying for.

Website development, technical SEO, content optimization, and ongoing SEO management are related, but they are not necessarily the same service.

## Final Thoughts

If your website isn't showing on Google, don't immediately assume that you need a complete redesign or an expensive SEO campaign.

Start by identifying the actual problem.

Is Google unaware of your website? Are your pages not indexed? Is something blocking crawling? Or is your website indexed but struggling to rank for the searches that matter to your business?

Each situation requires a different approach.

A properly configured website, useful content, clear page structure, and consistent SEO improvements can give your business a better opportunity to appear in relevant searches.

